VERDICT: PASS -- the one copper pluggable on the bench is an AT-SPTXa (1000BASE-T). It linked
         to the 4050 at every speed it supports: 10, 100 and 1000 Mb/s. The DUT refused 2500,
         10000 and 100000 Mb/s.
  Method (the database has no steps): read the module type. Set each supported speed on the
  DUT port, with auto-negotiation and as a fixed speed. For the forced rows (fixed speed and
  fixed duplex), set the 4050 port the same way. Expected: the status of both ports shows the
  set speed and duplex. The Link Layer Discovery Protocol (LLDP) neighbour is present.
  Speeds the module cannot run are refused.
  Where "supported speeds" comes from:
    - `show system pluggable detail` reads Device Name AT-SPTXa and Device Type 1000BASE-T,
      a 10/100/1000BASE-T copper small form-factor pluggable (SFP) (STEP 0).
    - The `speed ?` help lists every speed from 10 to 400000 for this port (STEP 1). So the
      help list is not the source.
    - The DUT accepted 10, 100 and 1000 (STEPS 2, 4, 5, 7). It refused 2500, 10000 and
      100000 (STEP 3).
      module type ................ AT-SPTXa, Device Type 1000BASE-T
      auto-negotiated 100/10/1000  3 of 3 connected at full duplex  (2.8-4.5 s)
      fixed 1000 ................. connected, full duplex, 1000  (4.5 s)
      forced 100/full, 10/full ... 2 of 2 connected at both ends; each end lists the other in LLDP
      partner agrees ............. yes, at auto 100, auto 10, forced 100/full, forced 10/full
                                   and auto (1000)
      unsupported speeds ......... 3 of 3 refused, and the link stayed up at 1000
      error lines ................ only the 3 refusals start with "% "

  The speed matrix. Every status below reads "connected". "s" is the time the DUT took to
